3. SCRAM
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Project:          SCRAM (a probabilistic risk analysis tool)
   Author:           Olzhas Rakhimov
   Upstream repo:    https://github.com/rakhimov/scram
   Licence:          GPL-3.0-or-later (LICENSE file present in the repository)
   Copyright:        Copyright (C) 2014-2018 Olzhas Rakhimov
   Commit ported
   from:             b85b78940de38996eeffec54d946824bd4280a1c (2019-07-03)
   Version reported: SCRAM 0.16.2
   Date accessed:    2026-09-21

   Ported into:      src/scram/probability.rs, src/scram/importance.rs,
                     src/scram/zbdd.rs, src/scram/expression.rs,
                     src/scram/mef.rs, the probability recurrence of
                     src/scram/bdd.rs, and the Connective TAXONOMY ONLY of
                     src/scram/fault_tree.rs.
   Those files carry their own per-file attribution headers naming the
   upstream file. src/scram/mod.rs is the module doc and re-export surface and
   carries the same header.

   WHAT WAS PORTED is the fault-tree QUANTIFICATION layer: the rare-event and
   MCUB approximations, the per-cut-set product, the five derived importance
   factors of `ImportanceAnalyzerBase::Analyze`, and `pdag.h`'s eight-variant
   `Connective` enum.

   WHAT IS IN THE MODULE BUT IS **NOT** A PORT, each saying so in its own
   doc rather than carrying an attribution header:

     - src/scram/mocus.rs. Upstream's MOCUS is a driver over a ZBDD operating
       on a preprocessed Boolean graph (zbdd + pdag + preprocessor + bdd =
       9,076 lines), none of which is ported. This is the classical top-down
       MOCUS expansion from the published literature (Fussell & Vesely 1972;
       cf. Rauzy 1993), verified AGAINST upstream's reported products.
     - The exact top-event probability: inclusion-exclusion where upstream
       traverses a BDD.
     - The Birnbaum importance factor: its definition, where upstream
       differentiates the BDD.
     - The rest of src/scram/fault_tree.rs: a plain indexed structure a
       caller builds in Rust. src/scram/mef.rs is what builds one from an
       input model.
     - RelaxNG schema validation. Upstream validates every document against
       share/input.rng with libxml2 before reading it; src/scram/mef.rs does
       not, and says so. Its structure checks are narrower than the grammar.

   STILL ABSENT: the preprocessor, event trees, and the reporter.

   ALIGNMENTS ARE present, in src/scram/alignment.rs, porting alignment.{h,cc}
   and the phase application of RiskAnalysis::RunAnalysis: a phase scales the
   mission time by its fraction and applies its <set-house-event>
   instructions. Upstream mutates the model and restores it with a
   scope_guard; MefModel::in_phase returns a new model instead.

   UNCERTAINTY ANALYSIS IS present, in src/scram/uncertainty.rs, porting
   uncertainty_analysis.{h,cc}: the Monte Carlo loop, the [0,1] clamp on a
   sampled probability, and every statistic including the n/(n-1) variance
   correction and the exp(1.96 sigma) error factor. Expression::sample ports
   Expression::Sample and each deviate's DoSample. THE RANDOM STREAM DIFFERS
   -- upstream uses one static std::mt19937, this uses the workspace's
   outram_mc_libs::rng::lcg -- so results agree in distribution and never
   sample for sample, and the verification is statistical. Upstream's
   histogram BIN EDGES are not reproduced (they come from boost::accumulators'
   density accumulator and are not documented as reproducible); the quantiles
   here are exact order statistics where upstream estimates them online.

   SUBSTITUTIONS ARE present, in src/scram/substitution.rs, porting
   substitution.{h,cc} together with Pdag::ConstructSubstitution (declarative
   substitutions, a tree rewrite) and Zbdd::ApplySubstitutions
   (non-declarative ones, a pass over the generated products).

   COMMON-CAUSE FAILURE GROUPS ARE present, in src/scram/ccf.rs, porting
   ccf_group.{h,cc}: all four models (beta-factor, MGL, alpha-factor,
   phi-factor), their CalculateProbabilities formulas, the combination
   reciprocal, and ApplyModel's proxy-gate rewrite with CcfEvent::MakeName's
   bracketed naming. THIS PORT APPLIES THEM BY DEFAULT, where upstream
   applies them only under `scram --ccf`; MefModel::without_ccf is the
   explicit ablation and both paths are verified against the corresponding
   upstream run. Each construct src/scram/mef.rs does not read is REFUSED
   with a reason rather than skipped.

   <define-extern-function> is refused DELIBERATELY AND PERMANENTLY: it loads
   a shared library named by the input file, which the workspace
   RESPONSIBLE_USE.md rule on autonomous access to systems forbids. This is a
   decision, not a gap.

   XML INPUT IS present, in src/scram/mef.rs, porting Initializer, xml,
   element, model, fault_tree and event -- the Model Exchange Format reader,
   including Initializer::GetEntity/GetEvent name resolution, mef::Id/Role
   public-and-private naming with <define-component> namespaces, <xi:include>,
   and Pdag::ConstructComplexGate's rewrites of <iff>, <imply> and
   <cardinality> into the eight analysis connectives. It uses the pure-Rust
   xml-rs pull parser rather than libxml2, so Android and wasm stay clean.

   THE EXPRESSION LIBRARY IS present, in src/scram/expression.rs, porting
   p_exp/p_exp2, the exponential, GLM, Weibull and periodic-test models, the
   numeric, Boolean and conditional operators, and all seven random deviates
   of src/expression/random_deviate.{h,cc} together with the Interval domain
   type. Only each deviate's DETERMINISTIC value() is ported; Expression::
   Sample() and each DoSample() land with the uncertainty analysis, whose
   `scram --uncertainty` output is the only oracle for them. The
   trigonometric operators and Switch are not ported, and appear in no
   upstream input model.

   HOUSE EVENTS are present, as Arg::Constant -- conditions fixed for the
   analysis rather than sampled, which the MEF writes as
   <constant value="true"/>.

   PRIME IMPLICANTS are present, in src/scram/zbdd.rs, porting
   Zbdd::ConvertBddPrimeImplicants together with Bdd::Consensus (which is
   Apply<kAnd> on an if-then-else node's two branches). Upstream's module
   handling and its EliminateComplements are not ported.

   A ZBDD IS present, in src/scram/zbdd.rs, porting Zbdd::ConvertBdd,
   Zbdd::Minimize and Zbdd::Subsume with attribution. Upstream's module
   handling, its ConvertBddPrimeImplicants path and its EliminateComplements
   are not ported.

   A BDD IS present, in src/scram/bdd.rs. Its probability recurrence is
   ported from ProbabilityAnalyzer<Bdd>::CalculateProbability and carries the
   attribution header; the diagram construction around it is not a port --
   upstream builds from a preprocessed Pdag, this builds from the tree
   directly by Bryant's standard algorithm.

   NON-COHERENT trees ARE handled: negated gates expand through their De
   Morgan duals, contradictory partial sets are dropped, and the complements
   are deleted and the result re-minimised -- which is what upstream's
   Zbdd::EliminateComplement plus Zbdd::Minimize do. Their minimal cut sets
   are conservative by definition, so quantifying them bounds the top-event
   probability from above rather than computing it.

   NOT absent, despite appearances: upstream's product probability cut-off.
   SCRAM 0.16.2 stores and validates `Settings::cut_off_` but never reads it
   (its getter has no callers), so no product is discarded by probability and
   there is nothing to port. Measured, not inferred -- see
   docs/scram-port-verification.md.

   UPSTREAM INPUT MODELS ARE REDISTRIBUTED, unchanged, under
   reference-data/scram/upstream-input/ -- fifteen .xml files from upstream's
   own input/ directory at the commit above. SCRAM is GPL-3.0-or-later and
   this repository is GPL-3.0-only, so the copy is same-licence. They are
   there so that the tests can read the models themselves rather than a
   transcription of them. reference-data/ is outside crates/ and so is never
   part of a published crate tarball.

   VERIFICATION: upstream was built from source and run; the oracles are in
   reference-data/scram/ with their own README, and the full record is in
   docs/scram-port-verification.md.


